    19	# Design analysis for the D4 variant
    20	# ----------------------------------
    21	# a) Concrete behavior and trace
    22	#    - The original task detects enclosed background holes and fills them.
    23	#    - In this variant the same border geometry remains, but every enclosed hole is already
    24	#      occupied by a solid non-bg interior color in the input. The output does not create a
    25	#      region from 0; it replaces the existing interior color with the palette-selected color.
    26	#    - Small trace:
    27	#      original input: border 4 around a 2-cell hole of 0, palette says 4 -> 6
    28	#      original output: those 0 cells become 6
    29	#      variant input: border 4 around a 2-cell block of placeholder 5, palette says 4 -> 6
    30	#      variant output: those 5 cells become 6
    31	#      difference: the actionable region is no longer "all enclosed bg", but "an enclosed
    32	#      monochrome island already surrounded by one border color".
    33	# b) Complexity calibration
    34	#    - To keep the visual rule obvious, all sprites still use irregular single-color borders.
    35	#    - Replaceable interiors share one placeholder color within a grid, so the user can see
    36	#      that solid interiors are being rewritten rather than discovered by color id arithmetic.
    37	#    - Exactly one distractor sprite keeps the same placeholder interior because its border
    38	#      color is absent from the palette mapping.
    39	# c) Old assumptions that break
    40	#    - The original `_fill_enclosed_bg_regions` assumes every target region is background.
    41	#      That fails immediately once interiors are non-zero.
    42	#    - The original adjacency tally over bg components is still useful in spirit, but now it
    43	#      must run over enclosed non-bg components. We therefore reject candidate regions that
    44	#      touch bg directly and only recolor components surrounded by one non-bg border color.
    45	# d) Placement legality
    46	#    - The generator still builds border masks with genuine enclosed holes first, because that
    47	#      guarantees the later solid interior region is inside the border and off the canvas edge.
    48	#    - Palette and sprites keep a one-cell gap through the existing occupancy mask, so palette
    49	#      detection remains stable and recolorable interior components never merge.
    50	# e) Visual observability
    51	#    - Inputs have zero enclosed bg holes for mapped sprites; their centers are visibly solid.
    52	#    - Outputs show the same geometry with only the interior color changed, making the D4
    53	#      change readable from a few examples without relying on hidden numeric ordering.
    54	# f) Visual cleanliness
